## 1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY & ARCHITECTURAL APPROACH
Deploying a multi-currency fiat-to-stablecoin payment processor across West Africa involves navigating two major challenges: fragmented inter-bank clearing rails (NGN NIBSS/NUBAN, GHS GhIPSS, XOF BCEAO/GIM-UEMOA) and strict AML/CFT/VASP regulatory regimes.

Building proprietary banking rails, independent clearing networks, and separate financial licenses in every jurisdiction from day one requires tens of millions of dollars and multi-year licensing delays. 

The institutional approach utilized by modern African cross-border fintechs is a Licensed BaaS (Banking-as-a-Service) & Regulated Liquidity Proxy Model:
1. **Fiat Rails & Local Inflows/Outflows:** Integrated via regulated tier-1 payment service providers and commercial banks holding local clearing licenses (e.g., Korapay, Flutterwave, Paystack, or Yellow Card across Nigeria, Ghana, Kenya, and UEMOA markets).
2. **Crypto & Stablecoin Infrastructure:** Built on dedicated MPC (Multi-Party Computation) custodial wallets (Fireblocks or BitGo API architecture) connected to institutional OTC desks for automated, zero-slippage USDT/USDC-to-fiat conversion.
3. **Card Issuing & Bill Payments:** Sourced via licensed card scheme infrastructure partners (Mastercard/Visa enablement bridges like Bridgecard or Maplerad).

