## 3. REGULATORY FRAMEWORK & LICENSING ROADMAP

Operating legally across West Africa requires a dual strategy: launching rapidly via authorized sponsorship licenses, while initiating the multi-stage corporate filings for proprietary recognition.

### Phase 1: Operational Launch (License Sponsorship Model)
* **Legal Setup:** Incorporation of an operating corporate holding entity paired with local operating entities. Drafting of compliant AML/CFT Policies, User Terms, Cross-Border Disclosures, and Data Protection Compliance (NDPR / African Data Privacy frameworks).
* **Operational Vehicle:** Partnering with accredited institutions holding:
  * **Nigeria:** Payment Solution Service Provider (PSSP) / Switching License and Approved Virtual Asset Service Provider (VASP) operators.
  * **Ghana:** Bank of Ghana (BoG) Payment Service Provider (PSP Enhanced) partner.
  * **UEMOA / Francophone Corridor:** BCEAO-accredited Electronic Money Institution (EMI) or microfinance banking partner.

### Phase 2: Proprietary Licensing & Direct Approvals

| Jurisdiction | Regulatory Body | Required License / Registration | Statutory Capital & Official Filing Estimates |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Nigeria** | Securities & Exchange Commission (SEC) | **VASP License** (Virtual Asset Service Provider) / ARIP | Minimum capital requirement (~₦1 Billion unimpaired capital) + SEC processing fees (~₦30M to ₦50M). |
| **Nigeria** | Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) | **PSSP / IMTO** (International Money Transfer Operator) | CBN statutory deposit (~$1M or ₦2B depending on tier) + licensing fees. |
| **Ghana** | Bank of Ghana (BoG) | **PSP Enhanced License** | GHS 2M to GHS 5M capital requirements + regulatory vetting. |
| **Global / Offshore** | Financial Services Commission (e.g., Seychelles / Mauritius / BVI) | **International VASP / Money Services License** | $25,000 to $60,000 legal formation, local agent, and statutory capital deposits. |

*(Note: Direct statutory capital reserves are company equity deposited with central banks, not software development costs. Most venture-backed and bootstrapped operations launch using Phase 1 sponsorship while funding Phase 2).*

